o be or  when it's going to be one year after he  urged his supporters to march on the  capitol former president trump is  expected to stay entirely out of view he  canceled a planned press conference  where he was expected to perpetuate the  lie about a stolen election many  republicans worried about the message  that would send on a day meant to  commemorate the deadly attack on  american democracy  donna  all right jackson in washington thanks  turmoil in kazakhstan what's fueling the  fierce public anger  [Music]  kazakhstan is not a country we report on  very often but there is upheaval going  on there that the world is watching  there are violent public protests  prompted by a sudden rise in fuel prices  a state of emergency has been declared  nationwide  for some context kazakhstan is a former  soviet republic and is geographically  huge about the size of western europe it  sits between russia and china and has a  population of about 19 million it's not  a democracy and had the same president  nor sultan nazarbayev for almost 30  years until he unexpectedly retired in  2019. the country's current president  has since reduced fuel prices dismissed  the government and removed nazarbayev  from an important national security role  but as redmond shannon explains it's  unclear if that will restore calm  this is kazakhstan in chaos violent  protests fueled by anger over the price  of gas  at the start of the month liquid  petroleum costs doubled after the  removal of a price cap the fuel is used  by many poorer kazaks to power their  cars  but the demonstrations continued even  after the cap was restored government  buildings were set on fire wednesday and  some security force personnel were  reportedly killed as yet there is no  sign of a large police crackdown but the  internet has been cut off during the  country's state of emergency so much is  uncertain  president kassium jomar tokyo says  financially motivated conspirators are  behind the violence  he's the hand-picked successor of  long-time authoritarian leader nur  sultan  much of the anger this week is directed  at the former dictator superficially  he's revered the capital was renamed in  his honor in 2019 and he remained in a  key role as chair of the country's  national security council tokayev took  over the job wednesday in another  attempt to appease protesters experts  say it's unlikely to be enough you have  this country which is the size of  western europe but it the political  economy is only owned and controlled by  120 of these kind of elite families  analyst kate malenson says she's seen a  growing sense of injustice in kazakhstan  in recent years because the kazakh  government have decimated effectively  the systemic opposition in kazakhstan  there is now this kind of uncontrollable  spontaneous sort of massive protest  peacekeeping forces from former soviet  republics will now deploy to kazakhstan  to stabilize the situation russia keen  to see the status quo remain for a close  ally with huge mineral reserves some  russian media outlets are already  suggesting the crisis was stoked by  western powers something the white house  has denied redmond shannon global news  london  next have you chosen pets instead of  children the pope has some advice for  you  dogs have a whole lot of skills but not  every dog will save your life a thankful  hiker in croatia discovered his wood  eight-month-old north was hiking in the  mountains last week when his owner fell  and was unable to move fellow hikers  left to get help and north and alaska  and malamute curled up on the injured  man keeping him  warm for 13 hours when rescuers arrived  they said it proves friendship between  man and dog has no boundaries  pope francis had a different message  today in his audience at the vatican he  said those who choose pets instead of  children show a degree of selfishness  and that choice diminishes humanity the  decision to have kids of course is a  complex topic and as eric